course: world
lesson: 82
title: Heat and temperature: how things warm and cool
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Temperature tells us how hot or cold something is; we measure it with a thermometer in degrees Celsius (°C). Heat is energy that moves from a hotter thing to a colder thing, never the other way by itself.
explain: Heat keeps flowing until both things reach the same temperature. A cup of hot cocoa cools down to room temperature, and an ice cube from the freezer warms up and melts, because the room gives it heat.
explain: Heat travels in three ways. Conduction passes heat through touching materials, like a pan handle getting hot. Convection carries heat with moving air or water: warm air rises and cool air sinks. Radiation carries heat across empty space, as the Sun warms the Earth.
explain: Most things grow a little bigger when heated and shrink when cooled. The liquid in a thermometer expands and climbs up the thin tube as it gets warmer, and bridges have small gaps so they can grow on hot days.
explain: Cold is not a thing that flows. When metal feels cold, it is because the metal carries heat away from your hand quickly; a wooden bench at the same temperature feels less cold because wood carries heat away slowly.
fact: Temperature is a measure of how hot or cold something is.
fact: Heat always flows by itself from a hotter object to a colder one.
fact: Heat stops flowing when two things reach the same temperature.
fact: Conduction is heat passing through a material or between things that touch.
fact: Convection is heat carried by moving liquids or gases; warm air and warm water rise.
fact: Radiation carries heat through empty space, which is how sunlight warms the Earth.
fact: Most materials expand when they are heated and contract when they are cooled; metal expands more than glass.
fact: A liquid thermometer works because the liquid inside expands as it warms.
fact: Metal feels colder than wood at the same temperature because it conducts heat away from your skin faster.
fact: Metals conduct heat well; wood, plastic and still air conduct it poorly.
fact: Wool, feathers and blankets trap still air, so they slow the loss of heat; they make no heat of their own.
fact: Dark, dull surfaces absorb heat radiation better than white, shiny ones.
fact: A full bath of warm water holds more heat than a small cup of boiling water.
fact: Normal human body temperature is about 37 °C.
exercise: What does a thermometer measure? | How hot or cold something is | How heavy something is | How much water something holds | How fast something is moving
exercise: Which way does heat flow by itself? | From a hotter thing to a colder thing | From a colder thing to a hotter thing | From a bigger thing to a smaller thing | From a heavier thing to a lighter thing
exercise: A hot drink is left on the table. When does it stop cooling? | When it reaches the temperature of the room | When it reaches 0 °C, the freezing point | When all of its heat has gone away | When it has cooled for exactly one hour
exercise: A metal spoon in hot soup soon has a hot handle. How did the heat travel? | By conduction through the metal | By convection through the air | By radiation from the Sun | By the spoon making its own heat
exercise: Why is a heater usually placed low down in a room? | Warm air rises and spreads heat around the room | Heat can only move downwards to the floor | Cold air rises and must be warmed on the way | A heater works better when it is close to the ground
exercise: How does heat from the Sun reach the Earth through empty space? | By radiation | By conduction | By convection | By the wind
exercise: Why does the liquid in a thermometer rise when it gets warmer? | The liquid expands and needs more room | The liquid gets lighter and floats up | Warm air pushes the liquid up the tube | The glass shrinks and squeezes it up
exercise: Why do railway tracks and bridges have small gaps between their parts? | So they have room to expand on hot days | So rainwater can drain away quickly | So they are lighter and cheaper to build | So they bend more easily in strong wind
exercise: On a cold morning a metal railing feels colder than a wooden bench beside it. Why? | Metal takes heat from your hand faster | Metal is always at a lower temperature | Wood makes a little heat of its own | Metal soaks up cold from the air
exercise: Why are people in hot countries often dressed in white clothes? | White reflects heat radiation from the Sun | White clothes let cool air flow inside | White cloth makes a little cold of its own | White clothes are always made of thin cloth
exercise: What is normal human body temperature? | About 37 °C | About 20 °C | About 50 °C | About 100 °C
exercise: Which holds more heat altogether? | A full bath of warm water | A cup of boiling water | A spoon of boiling water | A drop of boiling water
exercise: Why does a lid on a pan help water to heat faster? | It stops warm air and steam carrying heat away | It adds more heat to the water from up above it | It pushes the cold out of the water and into the air | It makes the gas flame underneath burn much hotter
exercise: How does a woolly jumper keep you warm? | It traps air and slows the loss of your body heat | It makes heat of its own as you wear it | It soaks up heat from the room and gives it to you | It stops the cold from flowing into your body
test: You hold an ice cube in your hand. What really happens? | Heat flows from your hand into the ice | Cold flows from the ice into your hand | Heat flows from the ice into your hand | Nothing flows until the ice melts
test: Which is true about warm water in a lake in summer? | Warm water rises and stays near the top | Warm water sinks and stays at the bottom | Warm and cold water always mix evenly | The water warms from the bottom upwards
test: A tight metal lid will not come off a glass jar. Holding the lid under hot water helps. Why? | The metal lid expands and loosens | The hot water makes the glass soft | The glass expands more and pushes the lid | The heat melts the glue under the lid
test: A black car and a white car stand in the sun all day. Which one is hotter inside? | The black car, because it absorbs more radiation | The white car, because it absorbs more radiation | Both the same, because the sun is the same | The white car, because white keeps heat in
test: A cold drink is left in a warm room. What happens to it? | It warms up until it is as warm as the room | It stays cold because it started cold | It cools the room down until it is very cold | It warms up until it is hotter than the room
test: Why does a saucepan often have a plastic or wooden handle? | They conduct heat poorly, so the handle stays cool | They conduct heat well, so the food cooks fast | They are stronger than metal when they are hot | They make the pan heat up more evenly inside
test: In a room, where is the air usually warmest? | Near the ceiling | Near the floor | In the middle of the floor | Exactly the same everywhere
test: A tiny spark from a sparkler is far hotter than warm bathwater, yet it barely hurts your hand. Why? | The spark is so small it holds very little heat | The spark is not really hot at all, it is only bright | The spark is cold on the outside and hot only inside | Your hand is much hotter than the spark, so it wins
test: Which thing is the best way to keep ice cream cold on a picnic? | Wrap it in a thick blanket or a cool box | Put it in a thin metal tin out in the sun | Wrap it up tightly in black plastic in the sun | Leave the lid open so that the cold stays in
test: why does the metal bench feel colder than the wooden one when they're both out in the cold | Metal conducts heat away from your skin faster | Metal is always much colder than wood outside | Wood makes its own heat from the sun | Metal pulls cold in from the air around it
test: wearing black on a sunny day, is that gonna be hot | Yes, dark dull surfaces absorb heat radiation better | No, black reflects all the Sun's heat away | No, colour makes no difference to heat | Yes, black clothes make their own heat
test: Leo says a coat makes heat to keep u warm. true? | No, it slows heat escaping from your body | Yes, the cloth makes its own warmth as you move | Yes, but only thick wool coats can do that | No, a coat lets the cold flow into your body
test: my sister reckons cold flows into ur drink from the ice. right? | No, heat flows from the warm drink into the colder ice | Yes, cold always flows from ice into drinks | Yes, but only when the drink is fizzy | No, heat flows from the ice into the drink
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the flow of heat between two bodies? | It flows from the hotter to the colder until their temperatures are equal | It flows from the colder to the hotter without end | It flows only when both bodies are of metal | It flows in both directions in equal amounts
exam: Identify the designation of heat carried by moving liquids or gases. | Convection | Conduction | Radiation | Insulation
exam: It is given that a metal rail and a wooden bench stand at the same temperature. Ascertain the reason for which the metal feels colder to the touch. | The metal conducts heat away from the skin faster | The metal is at a lower temperature than the wood | The wood produces heat of its own | The metal absorbs cold from the air
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a full bath of warm water and a small cup of boiling water. | The bath holds more heat, though its temperature is lower | The cup holds more heat, since its temperature is higher | Both hold exactly the same quantity of heat | Neither holds any heat whatsoever
